  "query_preview": "..(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 515 AM EDT THU AUG 13 2026\n\nThe most current model data continues to suggest the dominance of\nthe southern upper Ridge into Kentucky over the weekend briefly\nlimiting convection. However, this dome oh higher heights\nretreats southwest in time for the new work week returning the\nstorm track closer to the state. Right now, SPC has continued a \nMarginal severe threat into Saturday for the eastern part of our \narea. Meanwhile, WPC continues to highlight excessive rain threats\nthrough Saturday - though the concern is much higher prior to the\nweekend. \n\nThe previous long term discussion follows:\n\nAt the onset of the long term period, eastern Kentucky will find \nitself in continued northwest flow aloft, sandwiched between \nridging over the southeastern CONUS and troughing over the \nNortheast. At the surface, an area of low pressure will sit over \nAtlantic Canada, with a long and poorly defined cold front \nextending back through the Mid Atlantic, just north of the \nforecast area. This front will sag south through the area on \nFriday, bringing a chance for additional convection. As in \nprevious days, the environment will support intense rainfall rates\nwith any convection that does pass through; LREF mean PW values \nrange from 1.8-2 inches on Friday afternoon, well within the 90th \npercentile or higher when compared to climatology, and given \nalready moist soils, flash flood risk could be higher than in \ntypical summer thunderstorms. WPC has maintained a Day 3 Marginal \n(1/4) flash flood risk for our area to account for this. \nAdditionally, at least moderate instability is likely to build \nahead of the front, which could potentially support strong \nconvection. Given some model guidance suggests the potential for \nadditional shortwave impulses within broader northwest flow aloft,\nadditional MCS type activity cannot be ruled out on Friday \n(although uncertainty remains high). That being said, upper level \nflow will be weak, with deep layer shear generally favored to \nremain under 30 kts across the forecast area, which would not \nconventionally support widespread severe weather. Even so, some \nmachine learning guidance has been comparatively more aggressive \nin highlighting eastern Kentucky as a potential location for \nadditional damaging winds. Given some of the signals in global \nmodels, it is still plausible that MCS activity could increase the\npotential for severe wind gusts Friday afternoon/evening, \nalthough confidence in this scenario remains very low. For now, \nNBM PoPs were maintained given a lack of baseline model agreement,\nbut with the caveat that rain chances/timing will require some \nrefining as uncertainty decreases.\n\nBy the weekend, the front will have passed through the forecast \narea. Meanwhile, the upper level ridge will continue to build into\nour area. This will usher in a period of hotter and drier \nweather, with high pressure working to suppress most convection \nthrough the weekend. Unfortunately, the aforementioned cold front \nwill do little to bring down heat or humidity. Behind the front, \ntemperatures will actually trend warmer in accordance with \nincreased upper level ridging (highs likely in the 90s), and \ndewpoints will remain in the 70s. Accordingly, apparent \ntemperatures will be warm, likely in the upper 90s or perhaps even\nthe low triple digits on Saturday and Sunday afternoons. \nOvernight temperatures will only provide limited relief given a \ncontinued moist environment; although radiative conditions will be\nideal, valleys may only end up being a few degrees cooler than \nridges at most, with typical valley fog formation possible \novernight. \n\nEarly next week will mark a return to more quasi zonal flow aloft\nas an upper level trough begins to dig toward Kentucky and \nridging retreats to the southwest, initiating a return to a \nsomewhat more active period. Notably, an approaching cold front on\nMonday will bring renewed shower and storm chances. In comparison\nto the previous cold front, the air mass behind this front will \nbe marginally drier, with dewpoints expected to fall into the mid \nto upper 60s in most locations. High temperatures will also be \naround 5 degrees cooler for most locations, marking a return to a \nmore typical August weather regime.",
